            236,
            299
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": "",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            300,
            303
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " This trait is implemented by default for many types and behaves similarly in",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            304,
            384
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " terms of inference of implementation to the [`Send`] and [`Sync`] traits. The",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            385,
            466
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " purpose of this trait is to encode what types are safe to cross a [`catch_unwind`]",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            467,
            553
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " boundary with no fear of unwind safety.",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            554,
            597
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": "",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            598,
            601
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " [`catch_unwind`]: ../../std/panic/fn.catch_unwind.html",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            602,
            660
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": "",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            661,
            664
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " ## What is unwind safety?",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            665,
            694
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": "",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            695,
            698
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " In Rust a function can \"return\" early if it either panics or calls a",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            699,
            771
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " function which transitively panics. This sort of control flow is not always",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            772,
            851
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " anticipated, and has the possibility of causing subtle bugs through a",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            852,
            925
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " combination of two critical components:",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            926,
            969
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": "",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            970,
            973
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " 1. A data structure is in a temporarily invalid state when the thread",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            974,
            1047
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": "    panics.",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1048,
            1062
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " 2. This broken invariant is then later observed.",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1063,
            1115
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": "",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1116,
            1119
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " Typically in Rust, it is difficult to perform step (2) because catching a",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1120,
            1197
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " panic involves either spawning a thread (which in turn makes it difficult",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1198,
            1275
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " to later witness broken invariants) or using the `catch_unwind` function in this",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1276,
            1360
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " module. Additionally, even if an invariant is witnessed, it typically isn't a",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1361,
            1442
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " problem in Rust because there are no uninitialized values (like in C or C++).",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1443,
            1524
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": "",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1525,
            1528
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " It is possible, however, for **logical** invariants to be broken in Rust,",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1529,
            1606
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " which can end up causing behavioral bugs. Another key aspect of unwind safety",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1607,
            1688
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " in Rust is that, in the absence of `unsafe` code, a panic cannot lead to",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1689,
            1765
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " memory unsafety.",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1766,
            1786
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": "",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1787,
            1790
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " That was a bit of a whirlwind tour of unwind safety, but for more information",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1791,
            1872
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " about unwind safety and how it applies to Rust, see an [associated RFC][rfc].",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1873,
            1954
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": "",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1955,
            1958
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " [rfc]: https://github.com/rust-lang/rfcs/blob/master/text/1236-stabilize-catch-panic.md",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1959,
            2050
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": "",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            2051,
            2054
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " ## What is `UnwindSafe`?",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            2055,
            2083
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": "",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            2084,
            2087
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " Now that we've got an idea of what unwind safety is in Rust, it's also",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            2088,
            2162
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " important to understand what this trait represents. As mentioned above, one",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            2163,
            2242
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " way to witness broken invariants is through the `catch_unwind` function in this",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            2243,
            2326
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " module as it allows catching a panic and then re-using the environment of",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            2327,
            2404
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " the closure.",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            2405,
            2421
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": "",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            2422,
            2425
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " Simply put, a type `T` implements `UnwindSafe` if it cannot easily allow",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            2426,
            2502
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " witnessing a broken invariant through the use of `catch_unwind` (catching a",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            2503,
            2582
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " panic). This trait is an auto trait, so it is automatically implemented for",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            2583,
            2662
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " many types, and it is also structurally composed (e.g., a struct is unwind",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            2663,
            2741
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " safe if all of its components are unwind safe).",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            2742,
            2793
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": "",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            2794,
            2797
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " Note, however, that this is not an unsafe trait, so there is not a succinct",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            2798,
            2877
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " contract that this trait is providing. Instead it is intended as more of a",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            2878,
            2956
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " \"speed bump\" to alert users of `catch_unwind` that broken invariants may be",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            2957,
            3036
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " witnessed and may need to be accounted for.",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            3037,
            3084
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": "",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            3085,
            3088
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " ## Who implements `UnwindSafe`?",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            3089,
            3124
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": "",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            3125,
            3128
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " Types such as `&mut T` and `&RefCell<T>` are examples which are **not**",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            3129,
            3204
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " unwind safe. The general idea is that any mutable state which can be shared",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            3205,
            3284
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " across `catch_unwind` is not unwind safe by default. This is because it is very",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            3285,
            3368
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " easy to witness a broken invariant outside of `catch_unwind` as the data is",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            3369,
            3448
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " simply accessed as usual.",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            3449,
            3478
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": "",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            3479,
            3482
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " Types like `&Mutex<T>`, however, are unwind safe because they implement",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            3483,
            3558
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " poisoning by default. They still allow witnessing a broken invariant, but",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            3559,
            3636
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " they already provide their own \"speed bumps\" to do so.",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            3637,
            3695
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": "",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            3696,
            3699
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " ## When should `UnwindSafe` be used?",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            3700,
            3740
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": "",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            3741,
            3744
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " It is not intended that most types or functions need to worry about this trait.",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            3745,
            3828
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " It is only used as a bound on the `catch_unwind` function and as mentioned",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            3829,
            3907
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " above, the lack of `unsafe` means it is mostly an advisory. The",
